{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>Sell me first your birthright.</b> In tractate Sanhedrin, chapter Ḥelek (110b), Rabbi Akiva says: the ten tribes will never return, as it says (Deuteronomy 29:27) ״וַיַּשְׁלִיכֵם אֶל אֶרֶץ אַחֶרֶת כַּיּוֹם הַזֶּה״ — just as the day goes and does not return, so too the ten tribes. So too Jacob said, ״מִכְרָה כַּיּוֹם״ — that you shall not change your mind: just as this day goes and does not return, so you shall not change your mind; and likewise ״הִשָּׁבְעָה לִּי כַּיּוֹם״. And there are those who say that he said ״מִכְרָה כַּיּוֹם״ according to the value of the birthright on that day, for at that time the principal prerogative of the birthright was the priestly service of the firstborn, and that service was slight in Esau's eyes, so he was willing to let it slip from his hand. Therefore he said ״כַּיּוֹם״ — like one who acquires a thing that exists even on that day, and this is the priestly service, to exclude the inheritance, which has not yet come into the world — for what will be, who can tell him?" "And in Toldot Yitzḥak, and likewise in the book Olat Shabbat, I found that he said ״מִכְרָה כַּיּוֹם״ — like the birthright's worth today, for there is no time for taking a double portion in his father's property except after his father's death, and perhaps Isaac will have no property at that time, and if so the birthright is worth nothing at all; therefore it is fitting to sell the birthright for a small thing, like one who acquires a thing that is in doubt — for perhaps Isaac will have property or perhaps he will not. Therefore the sale should not be like one who acquires for tomorrow, but like one who acquires today. And to all these explanations it is difficult: what is ״כַּיּוֹם״? It should have said ״כְּהַיּוֹם״! And what I have written is the principal matter. Another thing: since a person cannot acquire a thing that has not yet come into the world, therefore he said ״מִכְרָה כַּיּוֹם״ — meaning, from today and onward after death, and ״הִשָּׁבְעָה לִּי כַּיּוֹם״ — that you shall not change your mind on the grounds that the thing was not in the world, but rather as one who sells from today and as though it exists in full on this day. And according to the words of the commentators whom we mentioned, his saying ״הִשָּׁבְעָה לִּי כַּיּוֹם״ does not fit at all."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {1 "The work 'Toldot Yitzḥak' refers to Rabbi Isaac Abarbanel's commentary; 'Olat Shabbat' is a collection of midrashic interpretations. The phrase הֲוָה לֵיהּ לְמֵימַר is Aramaic, standard in rabbinic textual argument meaning 'it should have said.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:30:18.537146602Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}